          "court": "United States Court of Appeals for the Ninth Circuit",
          "year": 2012,
          "date_decided": "2012-05-04",
          "holding": "Reversing dismissal in substantial part, the court held that foster children stated substantive due process claims under both the special-relationship and state-created-danger doctrines, and separately analyzed which Child Welfare Act and CAPTA provisions create rights enforceable under § 1983.",
          "relevance": "Shows the narrow set of Child Welfare Act provisions that survive Suter and Blessing as privately enforceable rights.",
          "quote": "we have already held that the state-created danger doctrine applies to placing a foster child in a home where there is a known danger of abuse",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/799337/henry-a-v-willden/",
